      "id": "chirps-v3-daily-rnl",
      "title": "CHIRPS v3.0 global daily precipitation (ERA5-disaggregated)",
      "version": "v3.0",
      "series": {
        "name": "Climate Hazards Center InfraRed Precipitation with Station data (CHIRPS)",
        "url": "https://www.chc.ucsb.edu/data/chirps"
      },
      "description": "CHIRPS v3.0 daily precipitation on a global 0.05-degree grid spanning 60 S to 60 N, redistributed as a chunked, compressed Zarr v3 datacube for bounding-box and time-range queries. CHIRPS blends thermal-infrared cold-cloud-duration estimates with a monthly precipitation climatology and in-situ station observations. It is fundamentally a pentad product: these daily values are the pentad totals disaggregated using ECMWF ERA5 reanalysis precipitation (the 'rnl' flavour). ERA5 covers the whole CHIRPS period, so this flavour reaches back to 1981. Values are daily precipitation totals in millimetres; ocean and out-of-domain cells are nodata. This redistribution changes only the storage layout and numerical precision, never the underlying estimates.",
      "license": "CC-BY-4.0",
      "resource_type": "dataset",
      "keywords": [
        "precipitation",
        "rainfall",
        "daily",
        "gridded",
        "satellite",
        "CHIRPS",
        "drought monitoring",
        "climate monitoring",
        "analysis-ready data"
      ],
      "note": "This is the 'rnl' daily flavour. CHIRPS v3 also publishes 'sat', the same pentad totals disaggregated with NASA IMERG Late V07 satellite precipitation and starting in 1998. The two are different products whose daily values differ by millimetres; do not concatenate or compare them as if they were one record. Values are stored as int16 in units of 0.1 mm (scale_factor 0.1), so every reported value is a multiple of 0.1 mm and differs from the source GeoTIFF by at most 0.05 mm, below the 0.1 mm reporting resolution of the gauges CHIRPS is calibrated against. Chunking is time-contiguous, making long time series over a region fast and whole-globe single-day slices slow; use a per-day raster product for global daily maps.",
